Grove PATCH-23: give BCH's overridden methods their electrum branches, and watch swap locks at the right scripthash

PATCH-18 brought BCH electrum (light) mode up on the Grove. Six defects in it
were then found by running the same feature against MAINNET with BCH in
electrum mode -- none of which lint, type-checks or the offline tests could
surface, because all six are "this method reaches an RPC node that light mode
does not have" or "this hash is computed for the wrong script type", and both
shapes are perfectly valid Python that passes every test not wired to a live
chain. Five of the six are fixed here; the sixth (coin_name_map) was PATCH-21.

The provenance is the upstream-facing BCH electrum branch, where these were
found and verified live. They are ported, not backported: v0.18.5 already has
useBackend()/getBackend()/_cached_wallet_info on BTCInterface, so the code
applies as written.

  THE COMMON CAUSE, worth stating once
      BCHInterface OVERRIDES each of these methods -- it does not inherit
      BTCInterface's version. BTCInterface grew an electrum branch at the top
      of each; the BCH overrides were written against the RPC-mode shape and
      never grew one. So in electrum mode every call fell straight through to
      an `rpc`/`rpc_wallet` that has no node to answer it. An override silently
      opts out of every fix later made to the method it overrides, and nothing
      reports that: the override is valid, the parent is correct, and the two
      are only wrong together.

  bch.py :: importWatchOnlyAddress    <-- the fund-affecting one
      Computed a P2PKH scripthash UNCONDITIONALLY. Swap lock addresses are
      P2SH, so every lock was watched at the scripthash of a script it does not
      have: the ElectrumX subscription never fires, the funding transaction is
      never observed, and the swap stalls after the coins are already sent.
      _destScriptForAddress -- added by PATCH-18 in this same file, ~15 lines
      below, precisely because cashaddr encodes the P2PKH/P2SH type in its
      payload -- already does the dispatch, and simply was not called here.
      Verified against Fulcrum's own blockchain.address.get_scripthash for
      P2PKH, P2SH20 and P2SH32.

      This is the half-wired-site shape the 0020/0021 series is named for: the
      helper existed and the call site did not use it. Each half reads correct.

  bch.py :: getSpendableBalance
      Observed live failing every 10 seconds with
      "Connection refused, method: getbalance". Now reads the cached wallet
      info, matching btc.py's own branch exactly.

  bch.py :: getUnspentsByAddr
      listunspent needs a loaded RPC wallet. Now reads per-address balances
      from the backend via WalletManager.getAllAddresses.

  bch.py :: withdrawCoin
      Now funds through createRawFundedTransaction (cashaddr) and signs through
      signTxWithWallet (FORKID), then broadcasts via the backend. Deliberately
      NOT delegating to btc.py's electrum withdraw, which is segwit-oriented
      and would build a transaction BCH rejects. Note this is also the path the
      sidecar interface fee sweeps on (js_bids -> withdrawCoin), so a BCH-leg
      fee collection depends on this branch existing.

  bch.py :: getBLockTxo
      gettxout is a NODE rpc, unavailable in light mode. Now fetches the raw
      lock tx from the backend and locates the output with findOutput against
      the expected scriptPubKey, rather than trusting the caller's vout index.

  bch.py :: getSCLockScriptAddress
      Called rpc_wallet("importaddress"). That call is not dead code in
      electrum mode -- isAddressMine IS backend-aware, so the guard above it
      evaluates normally and the RPC always fires. Routed through
      importWatchOnlyAddress, which registers the subscription the lock needs.

  electrumx.py
      Dropped bch.soul-dev.com from the bitcoincash server list: it resolves
      but never completes a TLS handshake (3 attempts, 20s timeouts each), so
      it burned a full connect timeout on every rotation. The remaining 12 are
      reachable and agree on the chain tip.

Applies on top of PATCH-22. Touches basicswap/, so it counts toward the engine
patch level (p21 -> p22).

